  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 306 AM CDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nHigh pressure aloft will remain anchored over the southwestern \nCONUS through next week. Ridging will extend north of this \nfeature into the northern Rockies. The amplitude of the ridging \nwill be temporarily suppressed Sunday night into Monday night by\na strong trough of low pressure traversing southern Canada. As \nthis feature tracks east into the northern Great Lakes midweek, \nslightly cooler air will back into mainly eastern portions of \nthe forecast area. Further west and southwest, warm temperatures\nwill continue with daily highs reaching around 90 degrees. \nReadings in the northeastern forecast area will reach into the \nlow 80s Wednesday and Thursday. With the persistent \nnorthwesterly flow aloft, and favorable moisture at the surface,\nperiodic chances for showers and thunderstorms will continue \nthrough next week. As for the severe threat, deep layer shear of\n30+ KTS looks probable next week, favoring a continued threat \nfor severe storms daily. With thetae ridging across the western \nforecast area next week, storms appear to develop over the \nhigher terrain of eastern Wyoming and the western Panhandle \nalmost daily. Steering winds will carry this activity southeast \ninto western and central portions of the forecast area. Based on\nthe latest NBM forecast, precipitation chances are highest in \nthe western half of the forecast area, and this seems reasonable",
